<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">René Goulaine de Laudonnière</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1529–1574</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">René Goulaine de Laudonnière was a French Huguenot explorer who led the second French expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. In 1564 he built Fort Caroline near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-johns-river">St Johns River</a>, the centre of French Florida. In 1565 Spanish forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-menendez-de-aviles">Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</a> captured the fort and killed most of its garrison, but Laudonnière escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and wrote an account of the colony.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rene-goulaine-de-laudonniere.jpg" alt="René Goulaine de Laudonnière" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rouargue frères · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laudonniere.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
